1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
What is the phrase women in tech?

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:28,000
The phrase women in tech is something that's relatively new and I think most importantly it addresses the fact that there is a disparity between the amount of women who are in what would be considered technical roles and what might not be considered technical roles.

3
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:40,000
And in that sense it's quite inclusion based but can potentially nod to the inequality in the industry.

4
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:48,000
I feel in line with what you've said. Although for me women in tech just means, there's two ways I see, I look at it.

5
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Women in tech means more work needs to be done so that it's not called women in tech anymore, it's called people in tech.

6
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:07,000
And the second thing when it says women in tech it just gives me a sense of tribal community that we have each other supporting each other, lifting each other up.

7
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:11,000
So it could go both ways for me when you say women in tech.

8
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,000
I really like that perspective of it being a community within a community.

9
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,000
I think that's something that, a solidarity between women I find really powerful and that I think brings the inclusion.

10
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Do you feel supported when you hear that phrase or us sat here talking like this, do you feel supported?

11
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:36,000
I do, yeah. Even when we attend some conferences and there's all these women from different backgrounds but they're part of the world of tech.

12
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
And like you said, we know that there is gender disparity in this field.

13
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,000
And then to see that we're helping each other get to, with the support of the other gender as well.

14
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,000
It's not necessarily that it's all just women. There's so many men who are a part of women in tech as well.

15
00:01:51,000 --> 00:02:01,000
But the fact that everyone's getting together and have acknowledged the fact that there is a big gap and we're working towards reducing that gap is just really nice and heartwarming.

16
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:27,000
That's interesting that you mentioned that it's not just about women as well, because I suppose the fact that women in tech has become this term, whether or not it means anything to you, even if it was started by say a woman who maybe didn't feel so included, the men on the other side who have recognised this divide, the fact that the term has become so widely known means that they will be supporting it and doing something about it as well.

17
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:43,000
I think it's so important as well to have men supporting it because how can a minority bring themselves up to the level of the majority without the help of the majority?

18
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:52,000
There's no way that we can push past the glass ceiling without the men looking down, giving us a hand up.

19
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:53,320
Yeah, agreed.

20
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:09,000
I kind of think it's context dependent, whether it's a buzzword or not. But whether it is or not, I think it's for a good reason when used appropriately.

21
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:15,000
It's definitely not one of those phrases like, let's touch base. I mean, that means nothing to me anymore.

22
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,000
But women in tech, when I hear that, it does mean something. And if it's used appropriately, I feel it can be very powerful.

23
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:36,000
That's really interesting. Because it is, it has kind of become a buzzword. I think it's applied to, like your point about context, I think it's applied sometimes in situations where it potentially doesn't need to be mentioned.

24
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:54,000
And it's used in that sense to, I think, essentially say, we've got women here, so we're fine. We've checked our diversity question or whatever. But in itself, I don't find it, I suppose. No, I don't think I do think it's a buzzword. I don't think.

25
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:17,000
I think context is really important. Because when you think about things like International Women's Day, we're going to hear the phrase women in tech from left, right and centre today. And I think that in that context, it really dilutes the idea of women in tech. When it's used all year round, not so much.

26
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:25,000
That's really interesting. Make it a year round concept. Yeah, it's not just for a day. Yeah, it's not just International Women's Day.

27
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:33,000
It doesn't, the issues that women face in the tech industry don't disappear on March the 9th. And then start off again. Really? Yeah.

28
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:57,000
I think it's so important to have this as a focus all year round. That stops it from becoming a buzzword for me. I think it's platform dependent as well. I use Twitter a lot in work. And I engage a lot with women in tech on Twitter.

29
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:14,000
But the women in tech hashtag can sometimes just be added to say, the sharing of an article about a woman. And then it's women in tech. Well, yeah, it is. But it's not anything to do with this woman. She's just, she's just a woman doing a job.

30
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:19,000
She's just a woman doing a job. Yeah, that's just me. I get paid to do this. Yeah, that's really cool.

31
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:27,000
I get torn when I hear the word women in tech. There are times when I think, oh, yay, they're talking about something really important.

32
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:39,000
Or they need to talk about something which is very relevant to women in tech. And oftentimes when it's just used as a hashtag, it's just used so that companies are at the forefront that this is what we're doing for women on this particular day.

33
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:52,000
And then just fades away during the rest of the year. And then it makes me wonder, is this word another one of those fun words people throw around because they want to be included or they want to show that they're being inclusive endeavors?

34
00:05:52,000 --> 00:06:00,000
And are they actually doing something about it? So there are times that I've seen really good stuff come out of the when people say the word women in tech.

35
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:06,000
And then there are times when I'm like, no, this doesn't fit in here. Don't use it as much.

36
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:17,000
Well, it's a fully back right on buzzwords, particularly for International Women's Day, but it's a cliched phrase in a lot of sentences, but quite literally deeds, not words.

37
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:23,000
It's so powerful. Have a company that the company policy is to support women.

38
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Don't just put hashtag women in tech on each other page. Yes. That's not enough.

39
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,000
We need actual help. Yeah. We don't just need a hashtag.

40
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:35,000
No. I'll take a hashtag, but I'd like more as well. Yeah.

41
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,000
Please give me more. Yeah.

42
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:46,000
Yeah. You see so many of these lists as well from companies who are like, here are the top 10 women in tech.

43
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:51,000
Here are the top 10 women in web security. And it's like, okay, thanks for the list.

44
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:56,000
But what are you doing like for these women to support their work?

45
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:59,000
You've compiled a essentially Buzzfeed article. Yeah. Yeah.

46
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:01,000
You've got a list to call. That's great. Yeah.

47
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:05,000
How can we make this the top 100 or top 1000? How can we add? That's the whole point, right?

48
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:11,000
Maybe we have the top 10, but what about the rest? Yeah. How do we make this list bigger? That's the point.

49
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:17,000
And how do we get more women able to be on a path to get them towards that as well? Yeah.

50
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:32,000
I do. I do consider myself a woman in tech. Even though I'm not in a technical role as such.

51
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:39,000
I think that working at the Daily Swig, we have to understand a lot of technical work.

52
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:45,000
I mean, to a certain point. But I'm not from a traditional tech background.

53
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:54,000
But I do definitely see myself as a woman in tech. I feel a sense of pride in being a woman in tech.

54
00:07:54,000 --> 00:08:13,000
I think that it, for me, is a term that encompasses a lot of roles, not just your computer scientists and women with actual degrees.

55
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:21,000
So, yeah, I would say that I'm a non-technical woman in tech.

56
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:29,000
Nice. For me, I have a very different perspective. I consider myself a person at work rather than a woman in tech.

57
00:08:29,000 --> 00:08:38,000
I mean, the job I do wouldn't change, whether it's a male or a female or anyone else, respectively of the gender doing it.

58
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:42,000
It means doing and I'm glad I have the opportunity to do it.

59
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:48,000
I come into work not thinking I'm a woman in tech. I come into work thinking I've got a good day ahead of me.

60
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:49,000
Yeah.

61
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:55,000
Probably it's based on the fact that the phrase didn't exist when I started my career, maybe.

62
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:59,000
It didn't play a part in how I approached my career.

63
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:10,000
I do what I do because this is what I want to do, rather than I do this because I want to bridge the gap or I want to help the society or whatever.

64
00:09:10,000 --> 00:09:15,000
There are no big aspirations there. I just want to do what I like to do.

65
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:21,000
So probably a little bit controversial there. Not to say I'm not proud of being that minority.

66
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:26,000
I'll stand proud and I'll say I'll shout for mountain stops if it helps others.

67
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:33,000
If it can encourage one other person to take up and, you know, take that first step into this industry, I'd happily do it.

68
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:37,000
I'm really proud of that. But it doesn't impact my day to day life.

69
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:44,000
I like that perspective, actually. Just you don't think of yourself as a woman in tech. You think of yourself as a woman going to do your job.

70
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:45,000
That's it.

71
00:09:45,000 --> 00:09:55,000
I do really like that. But I guess the, not challenge as such, but the fact that you're in an environment now where you're able to consider yourself like that.

72
00:09:55,000 --> 00:09:59,000
I guess that's the next tier up for women in tech.

73
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:02,000
Possibly. I'm possibly in that sweet spot, so I don't need to think of this.

74
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:03,000
Yeah.

75
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:06,000
I can appreciate that for a lot of women. It's not the same.

76
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:15,000
That says a lot for the growth of the industry though. I think that there are women who are comfortable considering themselves simply people going about their roles in tech.

77
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:21,000
And it wouldn't matter. The output is indifferent based on gender, which I think is really powerful.

78
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:34,000
I think it's the end goal, really, to just exist without having to almost prove yourself and put yourself in a box and be like, this is who I am.

79
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:40,000
I am a woman in tech to just be someone enjoying the job and doing a good job at it.

80
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:45,000
And just being irrespective of gender.

81
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:47,000
You've climbed on top of the box.

82
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:48,000
Yeah.

83
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:49,000
That's impressive.

84
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,000
You own the box.

85
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:53,000
Maybe I created my own. I have no idea.

86
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:57,000
Maybe there's lots of stuff happening I'm ignoring and standing on my box.

87
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:58,000
That's it.

88
00:10:58,000 --> 00:11:01,000
Not that it's a soapbox. Let's put that one right out there.

89
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:02,000
Just a box.

90
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:03,000
Thank you.

91
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:05,000
Just stood on a box.

92
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:18,000
I think the only thing I'd add to this wonderful conversation, which already you all have had, it's just, I think when I came across the term women in tech, I was not entirely confident in saying that I am a woman in tech.

93
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:28,000
And that happened because of a couple of incidents when we attended some conferences or started introducing ourselves because I'm in a non-technical role as well.

94
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:35,000
And someone would ask me, so are you a woman in tech because you're here at this conference or you're doing this event or you're interviewing because of this?

95
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:36,000
And I said, yes, I am.

96
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:38,000
And he's like, so what is your technical role?

97
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,000
I said, well, I'm not a technical woman in tech.

98
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
I work in a tech industry.

99
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:48,000
And I said, yeah, but technically should people should be in this role or a woman in tech.

100
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
And I felt a bit excluded.

101
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:58,000
And it took me a while to embrace the fact that no, because I work in this industry, I work in the world of tech.

102
00:11:58,000 --> 00:11:59,000
I am a woman in tech.

103
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:12,000
So for me, the phrase resonates differently, perhaps to how it does to you because of the experiences I've had or because of my non-tech role being a woman in tech.

104
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:20,000
That's interesting that you were still shoehorned into a specific category even within this broader expression as well.

105
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:24,000
That shows there's probably still a lot to do, really.

106
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:30,000
My role is really balanced in that it encompasses some technical aspects and some non-technical.

107
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:34,000
From a traditional perspective, I'm not a developer.

108
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,000
I'm not a programmer, but I do build web pages.

109
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
I have interaction with developers.

110
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:43,000
So I think I would consider myself a woman in tech.

111
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:46,000
Maybe one day I'll learn JavaScript.

112
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:48,000
Big goal.

113
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:49,000
One day.

114
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
One day over there.

115
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:52,000
Yeah.

116
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:53,560
Yeah.

117
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:11,000
I definitely think that the phrase women in tech can be, can exclude women in non-technical roles for sure.

118
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:19,000
Even though I've said I do consider myself a woman in tech, I also sometimes feel excluded.

119
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:34,000
Mainly when I'm going to conferences and I meet other women in tech, as you said, and they are like, they're pen testers or they work in like cloud security.

120
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,000
And they're talking about all of these things that they do.

121
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
And I'm just like, I'm a journalist.

122
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:42,000
In a technical company.

123
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,000
In a technical company.

124
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:55,000
I definitely do feel an almost hesitation sometimes when talking to these people.

125
00:13:55,000 --> 00:14:04,000
I often caveat my own role and my own expertise with, I'm not technical, but I am a tech journalist.

126
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:08,000
Um, and I don't know.

127
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:11,000
I don't know why I feel like that.

128
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:17,000
There's not been any specific experience that I've had where someone's told me that I'm not a woman in tech.

129
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:28,000
Um, but there's just a general feeling sometimes that I get where I'm a bit hesitant to put it out there.

130
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:43,000
Um, and almost sometimes, almost a little bit ashamed of not fulfilling the technical, like CV of a tech worker.

131
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:48,000
Um, but I don't know if that's my own issues.

132
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:49,000
I'd agree.

133
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
I, I feel similarly.

134
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:55,000
My, my background's in English literature, in writing poetry.

135
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:58,000
That's, that, that's not the same.

136
00:14:58,000 --> 00:14:59,000
It's not the same as writing code.

137
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
It's a completely different mindset.

138
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:06,000
And it, it's hard to feel like your contributions are valid.

139
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:12,000
I think I don't always feel comfortable, like putting myself out there to ask the question and just go, I don't, I don't understand.

140
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:14,000
I don't know what this means.

141
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:15,000
I can understand it.

142
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
It's not that I'm incapable.

143
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:18,000
I just don't right now.

144
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:25,000
So I think confidence, having the confidence to feel comfortable, just saying, I accept, I don't know this.

145
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:26,000
I'm really interested to learn.

146
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:33,000
And it's, I think that's what's so important about being in an environment where you can ask those questions.

147
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:34,000
Indeed.

148
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:40,000
You can, you can highlight not necessarily your ignorance, but the fact that you haven't had the opportunity to learn that yet.

149
00:15:40,000 --> 00:15:46,000
And I think that's the balance for me is it's not just saying I'm ignorant and I will continue being that way.

150
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
It's going, no, I'm not ignorant.

151
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:49,000
I just, I haven't learned it yet.

152
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
It's there for me to learn.

153
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:58,000
I think it's really important to remember as well that nobody knows everything about web security.

154
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:02,000
Like who knows every single aspect of it.

155
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
It's so multifaceted.

156
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
No one knows everything.

157
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
There are always gaps in people's knowledge.

158
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:13,000
I might know something that you don't and vice versa.

159
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:16,000
There's always knowledge to be shared.

160
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:22,000
And the industry is evolving so quickly as well as new technologies constantly.

161
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
So it's so fast paced.

162
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:28,000
I can add a slightly different perspective to this if it helps.

163
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:31,000
I'm in a technical role, but I don't write any code.

164
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:36,000
But I do consider myself technical enough because I know I can do my job well.

165
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
And this doesn't come from pride.

166
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:42,000
It's with all the modesty I can gather right now.

167
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:47,000
I can say it's what's needed to be successful in your job.

168
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:54,000
It doesn't matter whether you can write code or whether you've got a degree to support the fact that whether you're technical or not technical.

169
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:55,000
My degree is in economics.

170
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:59,000
It's got nothing to do with what I do right now at work.

171
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:04,000
But I still feel, yes, I do fall into that category of being a woman in tech,

172
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
although I don't recognize it myself every day.

173
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
I do fall into that category.

174
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:12,000
So I guess there's a slightly different perspective to look at it.

175
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
So that's really interesting.

176
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:19,000
There is so many different roles as well in a tech company that need to be fulfilled.

177
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:20,000
Exactly.

178
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:26,000
If everybody was writing code, there'd be nobody to, I don't know, market the product.

179
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:30,000
There'd be nobody to speak to the customers.

180
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
It just wouldn't work.

181
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:39,000
And I think what's interesting about all of our roles is that they are wildly different, but they're just as important.

182
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:52,000
We have so much going on at Portsviga, from the actual products to the actual politics that we sell, to the Academy, to the Women in Tech Scholarship, to the Daily Swig.

183
00:17:52,000 --> 00:18:00,000
There's so many different parts of it that you can't just have technical workers.

184
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
Like it just wouldn't work.

185
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:07,000
And this is the point I'm trying to make, that we should all be very proud of the work we do.

186
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
Yeah, I agree.

187
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
Without us, a lot wouldn't happen.

188
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:12,000
That's true.

189
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:13,000
That's interesting.

190
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:14,000
Very true.

191
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
A lot would not happen without us.

192
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
That's a nice sense of power, actually.

193
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:19,000
Yeah.

194
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:23,000
That makes me feel more comfortable with my contribution.

195
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
I like that perspective.

196
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:26,000
Thank you for that.

197
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:31,000
Thank you.

198
